00:02:15.150 --> 00:02:17.219
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:02:17.519 --> 00:02:19.646
Nita, what have you achieved this year?

46
00:02:20.950 --> 00:02:24.656
I've passed four exams, and I've been practising English every day.

47
00:02:25.950 --> 00:02:28.927
That shows real dedication. How long have you been learning?

48
00:02:30.217 --> 00:02:33.984
I've been learning for three years. I've read twenty books in English!

49
00:02:35.283 --> 00:02:37.483
Wonderful. What's your next goal?

50
00:02:38.783 --> 00:02:42.562
To reach upper-intermediate. My reward will be a trip to Singapore!

51
00:02:43.850 --> 00:02:47.812
Grammar focus: Present perfect simple vs continuous.

52
00:02:48.112 --> 00:02:50.994
Use the present perfect simple for results and how many.

53
00:02:51.294 --> 00:02:54.651
Use the present perfect continuous for activities and how long.

54
00:02:54.951 --> 00:02:59.438
For example: I have read twenty books. I have been reading since nine o'clock.

55
00:02:59.738 --> 00:03:05.662
Use the simple form with numbers and finished results; use the continuous form to focus on duration.

56
00:03:06.650 --> 00:03:09.535
Now read and listen. Small Steps, Big Goals.

57
00:03:09.835 --> 00:03:13.831
Research shows that people who write their goals down are more likely to achieve them.

58
00:03:15.117 --> 00:03:18.219
Good goal setting breaks a big dream into small steps.

59
00:03:19.517 --> 00:03:23.490
Every failure teaches us something that brings us closer to success.

60
00:03:24.783 --> 00:03:28.118
Persistence and dedication are often more important than talent.

61
00:03:29.417 --> 00:03:32.368
Remember to give yourself a reward when you reach each step.

